**The located Exxon records establish laboratory processing, but do not identify either van as UMS or verify a pre-impact time.** I did not locate a playable original or authenticated copy, a final identification/exclusion, or an Exxon recorder-clock analysis.

Three findings matter most:

- **The lab result was inconclusive.** The October 18 examination of **K1966/AGBH009**, under **011001250 JD**, describes digitizing and enhancing selected frames, with “minimal” improvement. It states no vehicle identification or clock finding. The September 28 request’s **08:29–08:31** remains a displayed interval, not independently verified time. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="0"}
- **The strongest identification language is conditional.** The October 17 memo reports a “possible match.” It does not name K1966/AGBH009, so its connection to that particular specimen is inferred from the shared Exxon description. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="1"}
- **There are concrete retrieval targets:** the routing record lists **five sets of 13 output prints and six CD-Rs**, including New York/Newark copies and a file CD-R. These are described as image products; the record does not establish six complete video copies. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="2"}

The evidence located does not identify the footage’s occupants or establish corrected actual time. A vehicle match alone would not establish advance knowledge.

**Bayoumi–Awlaki:** the year remains unresolved. The 2022 report says **January 1999**; the 2024 averment says **January 1998**. Their matching **40:35** locator suggests the same embrace sequence, but authenticated file equivalence was not established. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="3"} A filing identifies **May 3, 2023 Youssef errata**—a useful next target—but I did not obtain its contents or the complete deposition. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="4"}

The six-page report includes original lab scans, witness/vehicle comparisons, clock distinctions, and exact source locators:

